NAME
  cckkssuumm	- Displays the checksum	and byte count of a file

SYNOPSIS

  cckkssuumm	[_f_i_l_e ...]

STANDARDS

  Interfaces documented	on this	reference page conform to industry standards
  as follows:

  cckkssuumm:  XPG4,	XPG4-UNIX

  Refer	to the ssttaannddaarrddss(5) reference page for more information	about indus-
  try standards	and associated tags.

FLAGS

  None

PARAMETERS

  _f_i_l_e	   The pathname	of a file to be	checked.

	   If no file is specified, standard input is used.

DESCRIPTION

  The cckkssuumm command reads the files specified by the _f_i_l_e argument and calcu-
  lates	a 32-bit checksum Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC)	and the	byte count
  for each file. If no files are specified, standard input is read. The
  checksum, number of bytes, and filename are written to standard output. If
  standard input is used, no pathname is printed.

  The cckkssuumm command can	be used	to compare a suspect file copied or communi-
  cated	over noisy transmission	lines against an exact copy of a trusted
  file.	The comparison made by the cckkssuumm command may not be cryptographically
  secure; however, it is unlikely that an accidentally damaged file will pro-
  duce the same	checksum as the	original file.

  [Digital]  The cckkssuumm command uses a different	algorithm than the ssuumm com-
  mand to calculate the	32-bit checksum	CRC.  The cckkssuumm	command	uses a CRC
  algorithm based on the Ethernet standard frame check.	 In addition, the sum
  block	count is an octet count	in cckkssuumm.

  [Digital]  The IEEE Std 1003.2-1992 and X/Open CAE specifications require a
  slightly different method of calculating checksum values.  To	obtain these
  new standards, set the environment variable CCMMDD__EENNVV to _x_p_g_4 as follows:

	eexxppoorrtt CCMMDD__EENNVV==xxppgg44


  [Digital]  To	maintain compatibility if the environment variable CCMMDD__EENNVV is
  not set, the default action of the cckkssuumm command reports the traditional
  checksum values.

  The CRC checksum is obtained in the following	way:

  The encoding is defined by the generating polynomial:

  _G(_x) = _x32 + _x26 + _x23 + _x22 + _x16 + _x12 + _x11 + _x10 + _x8 + _x7 + _x5 +	_x4 +
  _x2 + _x + 1

  Mathematically, the CRC value	corresponding to a given file is defined by
  the following	procedure:

   11..  The _n bits to be	evaluated are considered to be the coefficients	of a
       mod 2 polynomial	_M(_x) of	degree _n-1.  These _n bits are the bits from
       the file, with the most significant bit being the most significant bit
       of the first octet of the file and the last bit being the least signi-
       ficant bit of the last octet, padded with zero bits (if necessary) to
       achieve an integral number of octets, followed by one or	more octets
       representing the	length of the file as a	binary value, least signifi-
       cant octet first.  The smallest number of octets	capable	of represent-
       ing this	integer	is used.

   22..  _M(_x) is multiplied by _x32 (that is, shifted left	32 bits) and divided
       by _G(_x) using mod 2 division, producing a remainder _R(_x)	of degree
       less than or equal to 31.

   33..  The coefficients	of _R(_x)	are considered to be a 32-bit sequence.

   44..  The bit sequence	is complemented	and the	result is the CRC.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

  The following	environment variables affect the execution of cckkssuumm:

  CCMMDD__EENNVV  [Digital]  If this variable is set to _x_p_g_4, the method of calcu-
	   lating checksum values specified by IEEE Std	1003.2-1992 (POSIX.2)
	   and X/Open CAE specifications is used.

  LLAANNGG	   Provides a default value for	the internationalization variables
	   that	are unset or null. If LLAANNGG is unset or null, the correspond-
	   ing value from the default locale is	used. If any of	the interna-
	   tionalization variables contain an invalid setting, the utility
	   behaves as if none of the variables had been	defined.

  LLCC__AALLLL   If set to a non-empty string	value, overrides the values of all
	   the other internationalization variables.

  LLCC__CCTTYYPPEE Determines the locale for the interpretation	of sequences of	bytes
	   of text data	as characters (for example, single-byte	as opposed to
	   multi-byte characters in arguments).

  LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS
	   Determines the locale for the format	and contents of	diagnostic
	   messages written to standard	error.

  NNLLSSPPAATTHH  Determines the location of message catalogues for the processing
	   of LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS.

EXAMPLES

  To display the checksum and the size,	in bytes, of ffiillee11 and ffiillee22, enter:

       cckkssuumm ffiillee11 ffiillee22

       3995432187      1390    file1
       3266927833      20912   file2

  This output shows that the checksum of the ffiillee11 file	is 3995432187 and
  contains 1390	bytes, and that	the checksum of	the ffiillee22 file is 3266927833
  and contains 20912 bytes.

EXIT VALUES

  The following	exit values are	returned:

  00   All files	were processed successfully

  >>00  An error occurred

